<setup>
  <selectFromSequence name="a" from="0.6" to="0.8" step="0.1"/>
  <selectFromSequence name="k" from="2" to="4" step="1"/>
</setup>

<p>
  In this exercise, we use Taylor series to estimate the value of the definite integral 
  <me>
    \int_0^{$a} \sin(x^$k) \, dx
  </me>
</p>

<p>
  <ol marker="a.">
    <li>
      <p>
        First, determine the first four nonzero terms of the Taylor series for <m>f(x) = \sin(x^$k)</m> and state them in the provided blanks below.
      </p>
        <p>
          <m>f(x) = </m><answer>x^$k</answer><m>+</m><answer>-x^(3*$k)/3!</answer><m>+</m><answer>x^(5*$k)/5!</answer><m>+</m><answer>-x^(7*$k)/7!</answer><m>+ \cdots</m>
        </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        Use the Taylor series from (a) and the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus to express the value of the deinite integral as a series of real numbers.  State the first four nonzero terms of that expression, entering their values exactly.
      </p>
      <p>
          <m>\int_0^{$a} \sin(x^$k) \, dx = </m><answer>$a^($k+1)/($k+1)</answer><m>+</m><answer>-$a^(3*$k+1)/(3!*(3*$k+1))</answer><m>+</m><answer>$a^(5*$k+1)/(5!*(5*$k+1))</answer><m>+</m><answer>-$a^(7*$k+1)/(7!*(7*$k+1))</answer><m>+ \cdots</m>
        </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        Observe that the series that represents the value of the definite integral is an alternating series.  If we use only the sum of the first two terms of that series to estimate the value of <m>\int_0^{$a} \sin(x^$k) \, dx</m>, what is the maximum error of that approximation, according to the Alternating Series Estimation Theorem?
      </p>
      <p>
        The maximum error is at most <answer allowedErrorInNumbers="0.0000001" allowedErrorIsAbsolute>$a^(5*$k+1)/(5!*(5*$k+1))</answer>
      </p>
      <p>
        The value that results from computing the sum of the first two terms of the series in order to approximate the given definite integral is
      </p>
        <p>
          <m>\int_0^{$a}\sin(x^$k) \, dx \approx </m><answer allowedErrorInNumbers="0.0000001" allowedErrorIsAbsolute>$a^($k+1)/($k+1)-$a^(3*$k+1)/(3!*(3*$k+1))</answer>
        </p>      
    </li>
  </ol>
</p>